{
  "@context": "../../context.jsonld",
  "@type": "Process",
  "critical": false,
  "description": "Design of the shadow radiation shield that protects the crew, electronics, and propellant from reactor neutron and gamma radiation. A typical NTP shield is a layered structure placed between the reactor and the spacecraft: lithium hydride (LiH) moderates and captures fast neutrons, while tungsten or depleted uranium attenuates gamma rays. Shadow shielding only protects a cone behind the reactor, minimizing mass: a 1-3 tonne shield can reduce crew dose to <5 rem/year from a 1 GW reactor tens of meters away. Distinct from reactor vessel shielding (full surround for ground reactors — see energy.nuclear-fission); NTP optimizes purely for mass in a shadow geometry.",
  "early_win": false,
  "id": "space-propulsion.nuclear-thermal.radiation-shielding-engine",
  "level": "process",
  "name": "Radiation Shielding",
